n\nSlope -1/2: y = -0.5 x + c. For integer points, we need x even to get integer y? Actually if we write as 2y = -x + 2c => x + 2y = 2c. So line of form x + 2y = constant. For T_5, we need constant odd? Let's test constant = 5: x + 2y = 5 => possible integer solutions: y = (5-x)/2. For x odd, y integer. x=1 => y=2 => (1,2) sum=3 OK. x=3 => y=1 => (3,1) sum=4 OK. x=5 => y=0 not allowed (y≥1). So only two points. Not enough. Try constant = 7: x+2y=7 => x odd. x=1 => y=3 => (1,3) sum=4 OK. x=3 => y=2 => (3,2) sum=5 OK. x=5 => y=1 => (5,1) sum=6 OK. That's three points: (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). All in T_5. So line x+2y=7 works (slope -1/2). So we have three lines: y=x, y=-2x+7, x+2y=7. That's three lines each with 3 points. That's only three lines, but we need 5 lines. So we need two more lines each covering 3 points. Are there other lines that can contain 3 points? Let's search.\n\nPotential slopes: p/q with gcd(p,q)=1, p≠0, p≠-q, q>0. The bound says each line can contain at most floor((k+1)/2) = floor(6/2) = 3 points. So we need lines that contain exactly 3 points. The solution claims only three lines have this property for any odd k. But we just found for k=5, there are at least three lines (the same three as for k=3 but with different intercepts). Are there other lines? Let's try slope 2 (p=2,q=1). That's not allowed? p=2, q=1 => p+q=3 => |p+q|=3 >2, so bound says at most floor((k+1)/|p+q|) + 1 = floor(6/3)+1 = 2+1 = 3 points. So slope 2 lines could also have up to 3 points. Let's test line y = 2x + b. For points in T_5, x+y ≤ 6 => x + 2x + b ≤ 6 => 3x + b ≤ 6 => x ≤ (6-b)/3. For x≥1, we need b ≤ 3. Also y≥1 => 2x + b ≥ 1 => b ≥ 1 - 2x. For x=1, b ≥ -1. So b can be -1,0,1,2,3. Let's test b = -1: y = 2x -1. Points: x=1 => y=1 => (1,1) sum=2 OK. x=2 => y=3 => (2,3) sum=5 OK. x=3 => y=5 => (3,5) sum=8 >6 not allowed. So only two points. b=0: y=2x. x=1 => y=2 => (1,2) sum=3 OK. x=2 => y=4 => (2,4) sum=6 OK. x=3 => y=6 => sum=9 >6. So two points. b=1: y=2x+1 => x=1 => y=3 => (1,3) sum=4 OK. x=2 => y=5 => (2,5) sum=7 >6. So only one point. So slope 2 lines cannot have 3 points.\n\nWhat about slope -3? p=-3,q=1 => p+q=-2 => |p+q|=2, same bound as before. Let's test line y = -3x + b. For x=1 => y = b-3. Need y≥1 => b≥4. For x=2 => y = b-6 ≥1 => b≥7. For x=3 => y = b-9 ≥1 => b≥10. But also x+y ≤6 => x + (b-3x) ≤6 => b -2x ≤6 => b ≤6+2x. For x=1 => b ≤8. So b between 4 and 8. For x=2 => b ≤10. So b between 7 and 10. Intersection: b must satisfy both constraints for each x. Let's try b=7: points: x=1 => y=4 => (1,4) sum=5 OK. x=2 => y=1 => (2,1) sum=3 OK. x=3 => y=-2 not allowed. So only two points. b=8: x=1 => y=5 => (1,5) sum=6 OK. x=2 => y=2 => (2,2) sum=4 OK. x=3 => y=-1 not allowed. So two points. So slope -3 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 1/2? p=1,q=2 => p+q=3 => bound 3 points. Let's test line y = (1/2)x + b. Write as 2y = x + 2b => x = 2y - 2b. For integer points, x must be integer, so 2b must be integer. Let b = c/2, c integer. Then line is x = 2y - c. Points: x≥1, y≥1, x+y ≤6 => (2y - c) + y ≤6 => 3y ≤6 + c => y ≤ (6 + c)/3. Also x≥1 => 2y - c ≥1 => y ≥ (c+1)/2. For integer y. Let's try c=1 => b=0.5 => line x = 2y -1. Points: y=1 => x=1 => (1,1) sum=2 OK. y=2 => x=3 => (3,2) sum=5 OK. y=3 => x=5 => (5,3) sum=8 >6 not allowed. So two points. c=2 => b=1 => x = 2y -2. y=1 => x=0 not allowed. y=2 => x=2 => (2,2) sum=4 OK. y=3 => x=4 => (4,3) sum=7 >6. So one point. c=3 => b=1.5 => x = 2y -3. y=2 => x=1 => (1,2) sum=3 OK. y=3 => x=3 => (3,3) sum=6 OK. y=4 => x=5 => (5,4) sum=9 >6. So two points. So slope 1/2 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nThus for k=5, we have at least three lines with 3 points each (the ones with slopes 1, -2, -1/2). Are there any other lines with 3 points? Possibly lines with p+q = ±2 (i.e., slopes where p+q = ±2). The solution says there are three possibilities: p=q=1 (p+q=2), p=-2,q=1 (p+q=-1?), wait p=-2,q=1 => p+q=-1, absolute value 1, not 2. Actually they said |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (p+q=2), p=-2,q=1 (p+q=-1), p=-1,q=2 (p+q=1). That's contradictory: |p+q|=2 would require p+q = ±2. But p=-2,q=1 gives p+q=-1, absolute value 1. So they mis-wrote. Let's examine: The equation p x - q y = c, with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. The sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t. So the step size is p+q. For the bound to be floor((k+1)/2), they used |p+q|≥2? Actually they derived bound floor((k+1)/2) using |p+q|≥2? Let's check: They said number of admissible t is at most floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). This uses that |p+q|≥2? Actually they used that |p+q|≥2? Wait they said \"Because the line is sunny, p+q≠0; therefore the sum changes by at least |p+q|≥1 when t varies by one.\" Then they derived bound floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). This inequality holds because |p+q|≥2? No, they used that |p+q|≥2? Actually they used that |p+q|≥2? Let's examine: For any sunny line, p+q cannot be 0. But p+q could be ±1. In that case, the bound floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 = floor(k-1)+1 = k. That's larger than floor((k+1)/2). So they need to refine for |p+q|=1 case. They did: \"If |p+q|=1 then p+q=±1. The equality p+q=1 forces q≥2 (otherwise p=0 which would give a horizontal line). Because x=x0+qt must stay in the interval [1,k], the number of admissible t is at most floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2).\" So they argue that for |p+q|=1, the bound also holds. So overall bound holds for all sunny lines.\n\nThus the bound is correct.\n\nNow they claim that the only lines achieving the bound are those with |p+q|=2. Actually they claim that any line with |p+q|≥3 gives a bound strictly less than floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q|=1, they also get bound ≤ floor((k+1)/2). So the only lines that can achieve the bound are those with |p+q|=2. But they also need to consider the case |p+q|=1 with q=1? Wait they said if |p+q|=1 then q≥2, so the bound is ≤ floor((k+1)/2). But can it achieve equality? Let's examine: For |p+q|=1, p+q=±1. If q≥2, then the step size in x is q≥2, so the number of points in the column is limited. The bound they derived is floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For equality to hold, we need floor(k/q)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). For k odd, k=2m+1, floor((k+1)/2)=m+1. So we need floor((2m+1)/q)+1 = m+1 => floor((2m+1)/q) = m => (2m+1)/q ≥ m and m+1 => q ≤ (2m+1)/m = 2 + 1/m. So q ≤ 2 + 1/m. Since q integer ≥2, possibilities: q=2 for any m≥1; q=3 only if m=1 (k=3). Let's test q=2: p+q=±1 => p = -q ± 1 => p = -2+1 = -1 or p = -2-1 = -3. So p = -1 or -3. But p cannot be -q (i.e., -2) which is fine. So lines with p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2) or p=-3,q=2 (slope -3/2). Let's test p=-3,q=2: p+q = -1, |p+q|=1. Does this line achieve bound? Let's test for k=3 (m=1). T_3 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). The line with p=-3,q=2 corresponds to equation -3x - 2y = c? Actually p x - q y = c => -3x - 2y = c => 3x + 2y = -c. So line of form 3x + 2y = constant. Let's see if any such line contains 2 points (since floor((k+1)/2) = floor(4/2)=2). For k=3, each line must contain exactly 2 points. Does there exist a line 3x+2y = constant that contains 2 points in T_3? Let's test constant = 5: 3x+2y=5 => possible integer solutions: x=1 => 3+2y=5 => y=1 => (1,1). x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... Actually x=... x=... Let's solve: 3x+2y=5 => 2y = 5-3x => RHS must be even. For x=1 => RHS=2 => y=1 => (1,1). For x=3 => RHS=-4 => y=-2 not allowed. So only one point. constant=7: 3x+2y=7 => x=1 => 2y=4 => y=2 => (1,2). x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... x=... So only one point. constant=9: x=1 => 2y=6 => y=3 => (1,3). x=3 => 2y=0 => y=0 not allowed. So only one point. constant=8: x=2 => 6+2y=8 => y=1 => (2,1). So each constant yields at most one point. So p=-3,q=2 lines cannot have 2 points in T_3. So they don't achieve bound.\n\nThus the only lines achieving bound are those with |p+q|=2. But the solution incorrectly enumerated three possibilities for |p+q|=2: p=q=1 (p+q=2), p=-2,q=1 (p+q=-1), p=-1,q=2 (p+q=1). Actually p=-2,q=1 gives p+q=-1, absolute value 1, not 2. So they mis-identified. However, they might have meant |p+q|=1? Let's examine: For p=-2,q=1, p+q=-1 => |p+q|=1. For p=-1,q=2, p+q=1 => |p+q|=1. So they listed three possibilities: p=q=1 (|p+q|=2), p=-2,q=1 (|p+q|=1), p=-1,q=2 (|p+q|=1). So they claim these three lines are the only ones that can contain m+1 points. But we just saw that lines with p=-2,q=1 (slope -2) and p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2) indeed have |p+q|=1, not 2. So they are included as possibilities for achieving bound. So they consider both |p+q|=2 and |p+q|=1 lines that can achieve bound. Actually they earlier argued that for |p+q|=1, the bound holds but may be less than floor((k+1)/2). However, they later claim that lines with |p+q|=1 can also achieve the bound. Let's examine if that is possible: For k odd, floor((k+1)/2) = (k+1)/2. For k=2m+1, we need each line to contain m+1 points. For lines with |p+q|=1, the bound is floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). For equality, we need floor(k/q)+1 = (k+1)/2. For k=2m+1, this becomes floor((2m+1)/q) + 1 = m+1 => floor((2m+1)/q) = m. So we need (2m+1)/q ≥ m and m+1. This implies q ≤ (2m+1)/m = 2 + 1/m. So q can be 2 (for any m≥1) or q=3 only if m=1 (k=3). So for k≥5 (m≥2), q must be 2. So lines with q=2 and p+q=±1 (i.e., p = -1 or p = -3) are candidates. But p=-3 leads to slope -3/2, which we saw may not achieve bound for k=5? Let's test for k=5 (m=2). q=2, p+q=±1 => p = -1 (p+q=1) or p = -3 (p+q=-1). So lines: p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2) and p=-3,q=2 (slope -3/2). Does p=-3,q=2 line contain m+1 = 3 points in T_5? Let's test line -3x - 2y = c => 3x + 2y = -c. Write as 3x + 2y = C (C integer). We need points (x,y) in T_5 satisfying 3x+2y = C. Let's try to find C such that there are 3 points. For x from 1 to 5, y from 1 to 5, x+y ≤6. Let's enumerate points in T_5: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3); (4,1),(4,2); (5,1). Com